    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Built c. 2600 BCE over about 26 years as the tomb of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a>, it stood as the world’s tallest human-made structure for more than 3,700 years.</li><li>Its chambers carry no inscriptions but the painted names of work-gangs, yet those names, the Diary of Merer and the cemeteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a>’s family and officials tie it firmly to his reign.</li><li>Ancient Greek writers believed slaves built it; finds at the nearby workers’ camps suggest that thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> labourers did.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Rolls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> found at Wadi al-Jarf in 2013, the Diary of Merer, record a supervisor’s deliveries of white limestone from Tura to the pyramid in the 27th year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a>’s reign, naming it dozens of times by its original name, Akhet Khufu.</p></aside>
    </section>

